1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,399
What if you asked an AI a question, and it answered with a number instead of an essay?

2
00:00:05,449 --> 00:00:06,619
Meet Priya.

3
00:00:06,669 --> 00:00:11,167
Her shop gets hundreds of support emails, and each one needs sorting.

4
00:00:11,217 --> 00:00:12,659
Is it a complaint?

5
00:00:12,709 --> 00:00:14,461
Which team should get it?

6
00:00:14,511 --> 00:00:18,582
Google's new Gemini Decisions API does exactly that.

7
00:00:18,632 --> 00:00:20,335
It doesn't write replies.

8
00:00:20,385 --> 00:00:25,833
It answers yes or no, picks one option from a list, or scores against a rubric.

9
00:00:25,883 --> 00:00:31,095
And it can check up to one thousand questions against one input, in a single call.

10
00:00:31,145 --> 00:00:34,065
It costs ten cents per million input tokens.

11
00:00:34,115 --> 00:00:40,735
On Google's own tests, that's nearly five times cheaper than Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite.

12
00:00:40,785 --> 00:00:47,662
The good part: clean numbers your code can trust, and it reads audio, video and PDFs directly.

13
00:00:47,712 --> 00:00:49,159
Oh, hi Muja.

14
00:00:49,209 --> 00:00:50,443
Now the catch.

15
00:00:50,493 --> 00:00:58,543
TypeSafe's Jev is cheaper still, there's no caching or batch mode yet, and every benchmark comes from Google itself.

16
00:00:58,593 --> 00:01:06,153
My take: test it on your own data, run it side by side with what you use today for a week, and only then switch.

17
00:01:06,203 --> 00:01:08,695
It's a handy tool for one narrow job.

18
00:01:08,745 --> 00:01:11,654
The full pros and cons are on my blog.
